hello everyone welcome to the Laing  focused Channel and my name is Paul one  question that I'm frequently asked is  what language is most closely related to  English well some people would say Scots  but that's debated because other people  would say that Scots is a variety of  English leaving that debate aside if we  think of English and Scots together then  what language is most closely related to  them the answer is the frisian language  or maybe I should say the Frisian  languages in the plural Anglo frisian is  a sub branch of the West Germanic  languages it consists of the unlik  languages English and Scots or you can  think of this as just English if you  want and the Frisian languages a group  of closely related languages that  developed from old Frisian old Frisian  and Old English were very close to each  other but that doesn't mean that Frisian  and English are mutually intelligible  today English has changed dramatically  due to contact with other languages like  Norman French and the various varieties  of Frisian have been influenced by  Germanic languages in their respective  regions the most widely spoken frisian  language by far is west frisian which is  spoken by over 450,000 people mainly in  the free slum province of the  netherlands where it's co official with  dutch at the provincial level then  there's north frisian spoken in parts of  the state of schleswig-holstein in  northern germany it's spoken by fewer  than 10,000 people and is considered  endangered then there's saterland  frisian which is only spoken by a couple  of thousand people in one municipality  softer lumped in the lower saxony state  in germany saterland frisian is  seriously endangered as most of its  speakers are older  all three Frisian languages consist of a  number of dialects even saterland  frisian with three villages having their  own dialect frisian used to be spoken  over a much wider area of the north sea  coast  old frisian was spoken and written  throughout the fifteenth century but  lost its status as a written language  when rulers of Friesland made Dutch the  dominant language first under duke  albert the third of saxony then under  haps  rule then under an independent  Netherlands in which Holland was  dominant a quick sidenote  what's the difference between Holland  and Netherlands I thought they were the  same nope just like England is only one  part of the United Kingdom Holland is  only one part of the Netherlands  actually two parts the provinces of  North Holland and South Holland the  other provinces including Friesland are  part of the Netherlands but not part of  Holland so Holland became the most  influential part of the Netherlands and  Dutch remains the dominant language  frisian became mainly a spoken language  for a long time in this period coincides  with the middle frisian period over a  century later in the 17th century  Frisian poet gisbert  epics wrote poems and Frisian to revive  it as a literary language then Johannes  Hill artists began trying to standardize  written frisians orthography and  pronunciation while the optics wrote in  a more traditional literary style heal  artists tried to make the standard  language more similar to how people  actually spoke another Frisian yah-hey  Hulbert smih translated many plays and  books including the Bible into modern  West Frisian and this is what really  started the movement to preserve modern  West Frisian that movement picked up in  the early 20th century and continues  till this day so what is modern Frisian  like well that depends on which of the  three main varieties we're talking about  because they are distinct languages in  this video we'll focus on West Frisian  the most widely spoken frisian language  first some basic phrases in West Frisian  hello hi good morning gleamin  please Oishi blaze I'd like some coffee  please it should have a coffee Ola  thank you Tonka Ville thank you very  much Divac tongue note that unlike  English Frisian doesn't have dental  fricative sound like thumb as in English  thank you or the as in English that  excuse me never mean that they are  where's the restroom there is just hush  can I understand agree but I don't  understand a big Ibanez  I love Friesland Eckhart Humphries lon  holy [ __ ] Blake's gender  now let's examine some sentences in  Frisian the sentence means those are her  books don't mean a heartbroken as you  can see the syntax here is the same in  both languages and most of the words are  all cognate this one dinna may look  different but it resembles the word  being in English doesn't it this is a  form of the verb meaning to be which is  cognate with the English word but in  West Frisian the infinitive is that  which makes more sense if you look at  the past tense forms  Buchan is the plural form frisian has  two ways of forming the plural N or en  for nouns that have a stressed final  syllable and s for nouns that end in  unstressed syllables and also after  diminutive endings this sentence has a  possessive adjective but how was  possession shown if we specify who the  possessor is with a name for example  those are feni's books does being a  Finnish Buchan possession can be  expressed with a genitive ending on the  nail similar to English apostrophe s old  Frisian had a system of numerous  grammatical cases for nouns but just  like in English those case forms have  been lost except for the genitive but  the genitive case form has become much  less common - and now it's much more  common to use the word fond to express  possession dog banana bookends from  Fenna word for word it's those are the  books of Fenna notice that the definite  article the comes before the noun and  then faun comes after it another way  that might be even more common is this  Dodman Afeni had Buchan word for word  those are Fenna her books the possessive  adjective is used in place of the  genitive ending it's interesting that  these periphrastic ways of expressing  the genitive have become more common  than the genitive ending it seems to  show the continued erosion of the  frisian case system going back to this  sentence for a second dog banana bogans  from fauna note the definite article  there is one more definite article it  frisian has two grammatical genders  common gender and the neuter gender the  is used with singular common gender  nouns and with all plural nouns it is  used with singular neuter nouns book is  new  but it's in the plural form so it's  definite article is let's try making  that sentence singular that is feni's  book Bukovina notice that the definite  article is it because book is singular  neuter also as we saw before with the  plural sentence possession can be  expressed in those other ways to the  espheni's group this is possible but not  common anymore  daughters fan aha book is the most  common you may have noticed that the  demonstrative pronoun dot like that in  English is used for both singular and  plural in other words for both that and  those in west frisian there's a  distinction between demonstrative  pronouns and demonstrative adjectives  that the monster of pronouns are dipped  meaning this or these mister for both  genders and dot meaning that or those  also used for both genders demonstrative  pronouns are used as equivalents or  substitutes of the noun the monster of  adjectives are used to modify nouns and  come directly before them for common  gender singular and for all plural nouns  Deezer meaning this or these and d  meaning that or those and for neuter  singular nouns did meaning this and dot  meaning that here's a pair of sentences  to show you the distinction between  demonstrative pronouns and demonstrative  adjectives dot beanahab Oken de  Bougainville ofin ha  another sentence she has read 15 books  this year she hunted a year 15 book  imagine word for word its she has this  year 15 books read this sentence begins  with the personal pronoun she' which is  a cognate of English she basically all  the personal pronouns in West Frisian  are cognate equivalents of the ones in  English next we see the third-person  singular present tense conjugation of  the verb hava the present tense forms  correspond quite closely with the  english present tense forms but look at  the past tense forms in english the past  tense conjugations collapsed into a  single past tense form based on the  first and third person singular forms  but frisian still maintains distinct  past tense conjugations next we see did  year meaning this year year is a neuter  singular noun so the demonstrative  adjective is did rather than disa next  we see the number 15 obviously a cognate  with English 15 let's hear a few more  numbers ian tois TRAI fee our fee if  traitor all of these numbers are cognate  with English numbers but there's a  difference in numbers like 34 Fiona and  treati  literally four and thirty lazon is the  past participle form of the verb lazar  meaning to read Lazar has cognates in  numerous Germanic languages including  Dutch lazin and German lazin another  sentence this one means we usually stay  at home in the evening be blue an  emollient dish word-for-word 'it's we  stay normally in the evening at home the  verb Blio WA is a cognate of the English  word believe' which means stay or remain  but is obsolete in the standard language  normal is like normal or normally of  course of course you could say normally  in English as well making these two  sentences more similar units is the  genitive form of UHN which means evening  the genitive form of nouns is sometimes  used as a temporal adverb marker to form  adverbs of time finally the word 2 was  originally a contraction of two words  tah plus push this is the equivalent of  the Dutch  taos one final sentence almost everyone  went home early because of the storm  hasta el gouna engi Bettina was fun  ravenous to Adam word-for-word it's  almost everyone went early to home  because of the storm  this word el Canyon can be broken down  into two parts meaning precisely every  plus-one this word Ghia is the third  person singular past tense conjugation  of gayen the verb meaning to go in Old  English and an old Frisian it was gone  but heed one part of this word teed  means time the first part but is related  to a preposition B meaning by or at but  this is now an inseparable word fun way  this word means because of but can be  broken down into the parts meaning of  plus way though because of the spelling  of way I think this might be a loanword  from Dutch van witha finally stole  autumn is a singular common gender now  so what's definite article is the rather  than it hopefully you can see that West  Frisian is a language that shares much  in common with English but that also  shares a lot in common with other  Germanic languages sometimes seemingly  more so than with English but don't make  the mistake of thinking that West  Frisian is a dialect of Dutch or that  the other varieties of Frisian are  dialects of German they are distinct  languages that are worthy of recognition  and preservation the question of the day  for native speakers of any of the  Frisian languages how much do you use  Frisian in your day-to-day life and how  much do you use a different language  specifically Dutch or German and for  other people does West Frisian seem  similar to English to you what stood out  to you as being similar a special thanks  goes out to my patreon supporters  especially these wonderful people right  here on the screen  they are my top tier patreon supporters  so many extra special thanks to them as  always if you're interested in learning  Frisian one place you can start is learn  Frisian calm the guy who runs the site  actually provided the Frisian samples  for this video it's a free language  course something like duolingo for  Frisian so give it a try and to everyone  thank you for watching and have a nice  day  [Music]

